# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum VMO {
  enum CachePolicy: UInt32, Sendable {
    case cached = 0
    case uncached = 1
    case uncachedDevice = 2
    case writeCombining = 3
  }

  enum ChildOptions {
    static var slice: UInt32 { get }
    static var snapshotAtLeastOnWrite: UInt32 { get }
    static var noWrite: UInt32 { get }
  }

  static var zeroChildren: UInt32 { get }

  static func create(size: Int) throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func size(_ vmo: borrowing Handle) throws(Status) -> Int
  static func child(_ vmo: borrowing Handle, options: UInt32,
                    offset: UInt64, size: Int) throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func physical(resource: borrowing Handle, address: UInt64,
                       size: Int) throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func setContentSize(_ vmo: borrowing Handle,
                             _ size: UInt64) throws(Status)
  static func executable(_ vmo: consuming Handle,
                         vmex: borrowing Handle) throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func setCachePolicy(_ vmo: borrowing Handle,
                             _ policy: CachePolicy) throws(Status)
  static func map(_ vmo: borrowing Handle, offset: Int = 0, length: Int,
                  writable: Bool = true) throws(Status) -> Mapping
  static func setSize(_ vmo: borrowing Handle, _ size: Int) throws(Status)
  static var referenceChild: UInt32 { get }
}

struct Mapping: ~Copyable {
  let address: UnsafeMutableRawPointer
  let length: Int
  func load<T: FixedWidthInteger>(_: T.Type, at offset: Int) -> T
  func store<T: FixedWidthInteger>(_ value: T, at offset: Int)
  func read(at offset: Int, count: Int) -> [UInt8]
  func write(_ bytes: [UInt8], at offset: Int)
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A virtual memory object (VMO) is memory that handles share.
A process reaches a VMO's bytes through a mapping; there are no calls to read or write a VMO directly.

`create` returns a VMO of `size` bytes, rounded up to whole pages, zeroed (`vmo_create(2)`).
`size` returns its size.

`child` makes a child of `vmo` (`vmo_create_child(2)`).
`options` is one of the values of `ChildOptions`.
A `slice` shares the parent's pages (and, for a physical VMO, its cache policy), and while any slice or a mapping of it lives, the parent does not assert `zeroChildren`.
`snapshotAtLeastOnWrite` and `noWrite` are the kernel's other child options.
`zeroChildren` is the VMO signal that means it has no children: none was made, or the last one's last handle and mapping are gone.
Wait for it with `Handle.wait`; see sys-handle(2).

`physical` makes a VMO over physical memory, `address` and `size` page aligned, which an MMIO resource passed as `resource` must cover (`vmo_create_physical(2)`).
Its mappings are uncached device memory unless the cache policy is changed before the first.
`setContentSize` sets how many bytes `vmo` holds, at most its size: what a reader of its contents takes as its length (a warm boot's images, sys-system(2)); the bytes past it read as zero.

`executable` returns `vmo` able to run, its rights and `EXECUTE` (`vmo_replace_as_executable(2)`): `vmex` is a resource for the system resource's `SystemResource.vmex`, or the root resource.
`vmo` is gone whether it succeeds or not.
The launcher alone holds one, to start pushed builds (launcher(8)).

`setSize` sets a resizable VMO's size, in whole pages (`vmo_set_size(2)`): pages past the new size go, and the room it grows by reads as zeros.
`referenceChild` is the child option for a child that sees its parent's pages as they are, later changes included, as a pager VMO's reader needs (pager(2)).
Hosted, neither a size nor a child can be set (`notSupported`).

`setCachePolicy` sets how the VMO's mappings are cached, one of `CachePolicy`; it works only while the VMO has no mappings (`vmo_set_cache_policy(2)`).

`map` maps `length` bytes of the VMO from `offset`, a multiple of the page size, into this process, writable unless `writable` is false.
The `Mapping` unmaps itself when dropped.
It keeps the VMO alive.
Its `address` and `length` locate the memory.
The accessors check their range with a precondition and so trap on an offset outside the mapping.
`load` and `store` read or write one integer at a byte offset, with no alignment needed.
`read` copies `count` bytes out, and `write` copies a byte array in.

